# stellar-contrib-board — project rules

## Scope

- Testnet only. No mainnet code paths, no network switcher.
- No smart contracts. This level is Stellar Classic payments only.
- No auth, no database, no wallet-kit abstraction layer. Freighter directly.

## Honesty constraints — these matter more than features

- Payouts are manual and trust the pot holder. Never describe the app as
  automated, trustless, or decentralized in code comments, UI copy, or the
  README.
- Contribution data is a point-in-time snapshot, not live. Label it as such
  wherever shown.
- Do not invent contributor Stellar addresses. Ever. An unmapped contributor
  renders as disabled.

## Error handling is the deliverable

Every failure path must be reachable and produce a distinct, readable message.
Prefer an explicit handled error over a silent catch or a generic toast. If you
find yourself writing `catch {}`, stop and handle it.

## Commits

Commit after each numbered build step, with a real message describing what
changed. Target 14–16 commits across the build. Never squash the history into a
single commit, and never batch several steps into one commit to save time.

## Style

- TypeScript strict. No `any`.
- Small components, no file over ~150 lines.
- Do not add libraries beyond the stack listed without asking first.
- Do not scaffold tests, CI, Docker, or deployment config unless asked.

## No agent tells in the repo

This is a public repo that will be read by people. Write it the way a person
would.

- Commits: no `Co-Authored-By` trailers, no `Generated with` lines, no tool
  attribution of any kind. Plain imperative subject lines under ~60 chars
  (`handle unfunded destination with createAccount`). No emoji, no
  conventional-commit prefixes, no multi-paragraph bodies explaining what the
  diff already says.
- Comments: comment why, never what. Delete anything of the form
  `// Step 3: fetch the balance`, `// Now we handle the error case`,
  `// This function does X`. If the code needs narration to be readable,
  rewrite the code.
- No section-divider comment banners (`// ===== HELPERS =====`) and no
  `// TODO: implement later` left in shipped code.
- README and UI copy: plain declarative sentences. No emoji headers, no
  "Getting Started" fluff, no bulleted feature lists padded with adjectives,
  no "This project leverages...". Describe what it does and how to run it.
- No boilerplate that wasn't asked for: no LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING,
  CODE_OF_CONDUCT, or badge rows. No `.env.example` for env vars that don't
  exist.
- Variable naming: match the conventions already in the file. Don't rename
  existing things to be "clearer" mid-task.

## When uncertain

Ask rather than assume — particularly on Freighter API shapes, Horizon
response fields, and stroop/XLM unit conversion. A wrong guess in any of those
produces a bug that looks like a wallet problem and costs an hour to find.
